        <div class="c-article-body">
            <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Suppressing errors is the central challenge for useful quantum computing<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Preskill, J. Quantum computing in the NISQ era and beyond. Quantum 2, 79 (2018)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e660">1</a></sup>, requiring quantum error correction (QEC)<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shor, P. W. in Proc. 37th Conference on Foundations of Computer Science 56–65 (IEEE, 1996)." href="#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e664">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Steane, A. Multiple-particle interference and quantum error correction. Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A Math. Phys. Eng. Sci. 452, 2551–2577 (1996)." href="#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e664_1">3</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Dennis, E., Kitaev, A., Landahl, A. &amp; Preskill, J. Topological quantum memory. J. Math. Phys. 43, 4452–4505 (2002)." href="#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e664_2">4</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ryan-Anderson, C. et al. Implementing fault-tolerant entangling gates on the five-qubit code and the color code. Preprint at &#xA;                  https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01863&#xA;                  &#xA;                 (2022)." href="#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e664_3">5</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Google Quantum AI. Suppressing quantum errors by scaling a surface code logical qubit. Nature 614, 676–681 (2023)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e667">6</a></sup> for large-scale processing. However, the overhead in the realization of error-corrected ‘logical’ qubits, in which information is encoded across many physical qubits for redundancy<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Shor, P. W. in Proc. 37th Conference on Foundations of Computer Science 56–65 (IEEE, 1996)." href="#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e671">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Steane, A. Multiple-particle interference and quantum error correction. Proc. R. Soc. Lond. A Math. Phys. Eng. Sci. 452, 2551–2577 (1996)." href="#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e671_1">3</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Dennis, E., Kitaev, A., Landahl, A. &amp; Preskill, J. Topological quantum memory. J. Math. Phys. 43, 4452–4505 (2002)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e674">4</a></sup>, poses substantial challenges to large-scale logical quantum computing. Here we report the realization of a programmable quantum processor based on encoded logical qubits operating with up to 280 physical qubits. Using logical-level control and a zoned architecture in reconfigurable neutral-atom arrays<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Bluvstein, D. et al. A quantum processor based on coherent transport of entangled atom arrays. Nature 604, 451–456 (2022)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e678">7</a></sup>, our system combines high two-qubit gate fidelities<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Evered, S. J. et al. High-fidelity parallel entangling gates on a neutral-atom quantum computer. Nature 622, 268–272 (2023)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e682">8</a></sup>, arbitrary connectivity<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Bluvstein, D. et al. A quantum processor based on coherent transport of entangled atom arrays. Nature 604, 451–456 (2022)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e687">7</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Beugnon, J. et al. Two-dimensional transport and transfer of a single atomic qubit in optical tweezers. Nat. Phys. 3, 696–699 (2007)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e690">9</a></sup>, as well as fully programmable single-qubit rotations and mid-circuit readout<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Deist, E. et al. Mid-circuit cavity measurement in a neutral atom array. Phys. Rev. Lett. 129, 203602 (2022)." href="#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e694">10</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Singh, K. et al. Mid-circuit correction of correlated phase errors using an array of spectator qubits. Science 380, 1265–1269 (2023)." href="#ref-CR11" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e694_1">11</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Graham, T. M. et al. Midcircuit measurements on a single-species neutral alkali atom quantum processor. Phys. Rev. X 13, 041051 (2023)." href="#ref-CR12" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e694_2">12</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Ma, S. et al. High-fidelity gates and mid-circuit erasure conversion in an atomic qubit. Nature 622, 279–284 (2023)." href="#ref-CR13" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e694_3">13</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Lis, J. W. et al. Midcircuit operations using the omg architecture in neutral atom arrays. Phys. Rev. X 13, 041035 (2023)." href="#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e694_4">14</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 15" title="Norcia, M. A. et al. Midcircuit qubit measurement and rearrangement in a 171Yb atomic array. Phys. Rev. X 13, 041034 (2023)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e697">15</a></sup>. Operating this logical processor with various types of encoding, we demonstrate improvement of a two-qubit logic gate by scaling surface-code<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Google Quantum AI. Suppressing quantum errors by scaling a surface code logical qubit. Nature 614, 676–681 (2023)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e701">6</a></sup> distance from <i>d</i> = 3 to <i>d</i> = 7, preparation of colour-code qubits with break-even fidelities<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="Ryan-Anderson, C. et al. Implementing fault-tolerant entangling gates on the five-qubit code and the color code. Preprint at &#xA;                  https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.01863&#xA;                  &#xA;                 (2022)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e711">5</a></sup>, fault-tolerant creation of logical Greenberger–Horne–Zeilinger (GHZ) states and feedforward entanglement teleportation, as well as operation of 40 colour-code qubits. Finally, using 3D [[8,3,2]] code blocks<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 16" title="Campbell, E. T. The smallest interesting colour code. Earl T. Campbell &#xA;                  https://earltcampbell.com/2016/09/26/the-smallest-interesting-colour-code/&#xA;                  &#xA;                 (2016)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e716">16</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="Vasmer, M. &amp; Kubica, A. Morphing quantum codes. Phys. Rev. Appl. 10, 030319 (2022)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e719">17</a></sup>, we realize computationally complex sampling circuits<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Arute, F. et al. Quantum supremacy using a programmable superconducting processor. Nature 574, 505–510 (2019)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e723">18</a></sup> with up to 48 logical qubits entangled with hypercube connectivity<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 19" title="Kuriyattil, S., Hashizume, T., Bentsen, G. &amp; Daley, A. J. Onset of scrambling as a dynamical transition in tunable-range quantum circuits. PRX Quantum 4, 030325 (2023)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e727">19</a></sup> with 228 logical two-qubit gates and 48 logical CCZ gates<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 20" title="Bremner, M. J., Montanaro, A. &amp; Shepherd, D. J. Average-case complexity versus approximate simulation of commuting quantum computations. Phys. Rev. Lett. 117, 080501 (2016)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR20" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e731">20</a></sup>. We find that this logical encoding substantially improves algorithmic performance with error detection, outperforming physical-qubit fidelities at both cross-entropy benchmarking and quantum simulations of fast scrambling<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 21" title="Daley, A. J., Pichler, H., Schachenmayer, J. &amp; Zoller, P. Measuring entanglement growth in quench dynamics of bosons in an optical lattice. Phys. Rev. Lett. 109, 020505 (2012)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR21" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e735">21</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 22" title="Huang, H. Y. et al. Quantum advantage in learning from experiments. Science 376, 1182–1186 (2022)." href="/articles/s41586-023-06927-3#ref-CR22" id="ref-link-section-d28522313e738">22</a></sup>. These results herald the advent of early error-corrected quantum computation and chart a path towards large-scale logical processors.</p></div></div></section>

            
                
            

            
                
                    
        
